# 1️⃣ What is Indexing?

An **index** is a data structure that allows the DBMS to locate records **much faster** than scanning the entire table.

Without an index:

```text
Search Time ≈ O(n)
```

With a balanced index (B+ Tree):

```text
Search Time ≈ O(log n)
```

📌 **GATE Tip:** Indexes improve **read performance** but add overhead for **INSERT, UPDATE, and DELETE**.

---

# 2️⃣ Primary Index

A **Primary Index** is built on the **primary key**.

Example:

| RollNo | Name |
| -----: | ---- |
|    101 | Asha |
|    102 | Ravi |
|    103 | Neha |

Primary Index:

```text
101 → Block 1
102 → Block 2
103 → Block 3
```

### Characteristics

* Ordered data.
* One primary index per table.
* Usually sparse.

---

# 3️⃣ Secondary Index

A **Secondary Index** is built on a **non-primary attribute**.

Example:

| Dept | Student |
| ---- | ------- |
| CSE  | Asha    |
| CSE  | Neha    |
| ECE  | Ravi    |

Index:

```text
CSE → {Asha, Neha}
ECE → {Ravi}
```

Multiple secondary indexes can exist.

---

# 4️⃣ Dense vs Sparse Index

## Dense Index

Every search key has an index entry.

```text
101
102
103
104
```

Each key appears in the index.

### Advantages

* Faster search.

### Disadvantages

* More storage.

---

## Sparse Index

Only some search keys appear.

```text
101
104
107
```

Remaining keys are found by scanning the corresponding block.

### Advantages

* Smaller index.
* Less storage.

### Disadvantages

* Slightly slower search.

---

# 5️⃣ Dense vs Sparse Comparison

| Dense               | Sparse              |
| ------------------- | ------------------- |
| Entry for every key | Entry for some keys |
| Faster search       | Less storage        |
| Larger index        | Smaller index       |

📌 **Exam Tip:** Sparse indexes require the data file to be **sorted**.

---

# 6️⃣ B-Tree

A **B-Tree** is a balanced multi-way search tree.

Properties:

* All leaves at the same level.
* Keys stored in internal and leaf nodes.
* Supports efficient insertions and deletions.

Time Complexity:

```text
Search = O(log n)
Insert = O(log n)
Delete = O(log n)
```

---

# 7️⃣ B+ Tree

A **B+ Tree** is an improved version of the B-Tree.

### Characteristics

* Internal nodes store only keys.
* Actual records are stored only in leaf nodes.
* Leaf nodes are linked.

```text
          40
        /    \
      20      60
     /  \    /  \
 [10,20] [30,40] [50,60] [70,80]
```

Leaf nodes:

```text
10 ↔ 20 ↔ 30 ↔ 40 ↔ 50 ↔ 60 ↔ 70 ↔ 80
```

### Advantages

* Excellent range queries.
* Faster sequential access.
* Widely used in MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle, SQL Server.

📌 **Most GATE questions focus on B+ Trees rather than B-Trees.**

---

# 8️⃣ B-Tree vs B+ Tree

| B-Tree                        | B+ Tree              |
| ----------------------------- | -------------------- |
| Data in all nodes             | Data only in leaves  |
| Slower range queries          | Faster range queries |
| Leaves not necessarily linked | Leaves linked        |
| Less common                   | Most commonly used   |

---

# 9️⃣ Hashing

Hashing maps a key to a storage location using a **hash function**.

Example:

```text
Hash(Key) = Key mod 10
```

Key:

```text
37
```

Hash:

```text
37 mod 10 = 7
```

Record stored in **Bucket 7**.

---

# 🔟 Hash Collisions

A collision occurs when two keys map to the same bucket.

Example:

```text
17 mod 10 = 7
27 mod 10 = 7
```

Collision Resolution:

* Chaining
* Open Addressing
* Linear Probing
* Quadratic Probing
* Double Hashing

---

# 1️⃣1️⃣ B+ Tree vs Hashing

| B+ Tree                | Hashing                           |
| ---------------------- | --------------------------------- |
| Supports range queries | Excellent for exact-match queries |
| Ordered                | Unordered                         |
| O(log n) search        | Average O(1) search               |

📌 **Use B+ Trees for range searches, hashing for equality searches.**

---

# ⚠️ Common GATE Traps

### Trap 1

Primary Index is generally built on the **primary key**, not any random column.

### Trap 2

Sparse indexes require **sorted data**.

### Trap 3

B+ Trees store **records only in leaf nodes**.

### Trap 4

Hashing is **poor for range queries**.

### Trap 5

Dense indexes consume **more storage** than sparse indexes.

